1
00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:06.679
[S01] So I've been seeing this statement circulating online and it pretty much says you have about thirty-six months to make it.

2
00:00:06.820 --> 00:00:11.839
[S01] And it makes sense because AI will continue to replace jobs no matter how much people fight against it.

3
00:00:11.919 --> 00:00:18.879
[S01] Money as we know it will change or even cease to exist because millions of ASIs or artificial super intelligences

4
00:00:18.920 --> 00:00:23.339
[S01] will rapidly execute tasks beyond human comprehension dictating humanity's future.

5
00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:29.879
[S01] And aside the point, if you're interested in crypto, this is probably the last time you're going to see incredible gains

6
00:00:29.920 --> 00:00:36.109
[S01] because they're being regulated, they're being adopted, and after this, they're going to become more like stocks.

7
00:00:36.179 --> 00:00:40.039
[S01] Now, my intention with this video isn't to scare you or sound sensationalist.

8
00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:45.140
[S01] Everything I just read is things that I've been seeing circulating online. I'm indifferent.

9
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:47.979
[S01] I don't know whether it's true or whether it's false.

10
00:00:48.039 --> 00:00:51.979
[S01] So those aren't my opinions. That's just what we're here to talk about today.

11
00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:56.259
[S01] It can be absolutely wrong, and I expect most of it to be wrong.

12
00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:02.679
[S01] You may not have thirty-six months to make it, but in my eyes, you have about thirty-six months

13
00:01:02.719 --> 00:01:07.219
[S01] until the definition of making it changes drastically.

14
00:01:07.310 --> 00:01:12.019
[S01] Now, my intention with this video is to provide you with a perspective

15
00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:17.280
[S01] that will allow you to notice opportunities in this future that is coming very fast

16
00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:21.090
[S01] because it's quite obvious that something is changing, right?

17
00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:26.040
[S01] We're in this very noisy time on social media, on YouTube, everywhere.

18
00:01:26.099 --> 00:01:29.980
[S01] And usually what that means when there's a lot of conflict, a lot of chaos,

19
00:01:30.019 --> 00:01:35.620
[S01] that means that a new ordered structure is going to emerge and we don't know what that is yet.

20
00:01:35.719 --> 00:01:38.140
[S01] Thankfully, human nature remains pretty much the same.

21
00:01:38.219 --> 00:01:45.219
[S01] And I can't help but think that this period of rapid growth is marking a clear distinction between machines and humans.

22
00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:48.219
[S01] Those who lean into their humanity will thrive,

23
00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:53.140
[S01] and those who can't stop identifying with mechanical living, on the other hand, may not.

24
00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:58.819
[S01] So the first thing we need to talk about is the concept of doers versus directors

25
00:01:58.859 --> 00:02:03.780
[S01] because this is going to become extremely important whether AI takes over the world or not.

26
00:02:03.819 --> 00:02:06.180
[S01] Are you a doer or are you a director?

27
00:02:06.219 --> 00:02:12.580
[S01] So I want to set the scene with this with a very kind of famous Sam Altman quote.

28
00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:26.860
[S02] One thing is certain here.

29
00:02:26.919 --> 00:02:34.840
[S02] The amount of power that an individual has is vastly more now than it has been at any time in the past.

30
00:02:34.870 --> 00:02:37.599
[S02] And this has occurred in three layers.

31
00:02:37.629 --> 00:02:41.770
[S02] First, the internet gave people access to any and all knowledge.

32
00:02:41.800 --> 00:02:44.770
[S02] Power transferred away from schools and institutions.

33
00:02:44.800 --> 00:02:48.849
[S02] Second is that social media gave people the leverage to attract their own audience.

34
00:02:48.879 --> 00:02:53.620
[S02] Power transferred away from employers, publishers, centralized media, and even record labels.

35
00:02:53.650 --> 00:02:58.599
[S02] People can now learn, build, and earn without being dependent on someone else's authority.

36
00:02:58.629 --> 00:03:05.319
[S02] And third is artificial intelligence is giving people the ability to create, automate, and outsource almost anything.

37
00:03:05.349 --> 00:03:09.639
[S02] Power is transferring away from traditional gatekeepers and intermediaries.

38
00:03:09.669 --> 00:03:14.439
[S02] People will soon be able to operate at the level of large teams with a fraction of the manpower.

39
00:03:14.469 --> 00:03:18.969
[S02] Now, I know this isn't true for all situations, but this is largely true.

40
00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:22.120
[S02] The internet gave you an insane amount of power.

41
00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:27.909
[S02] And if you were born in the digital first age, like Gen Z was, or like me, who's kind of a zillennial.

42
00:03:27.939 --> 00:03:28.870
[S02] I heard that yesterday.

43
00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:32.050
[S02] It's like the people born from 1995 to 2000.

44
00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:37.719
[S02] It's like you're in between millennials and Gen Z, where you're kind of still digital first.

45
00:03:37.750 --> 00:03:43.180
[S02] But Gen Z people are obviously those who have grown up with this technology and they're leveraging it.

46
00:03:43.210 --> 00:03:48.400
[S02] And their lives are both substantially better and substantially worse from this

47
00:03:48.430 --> 00:03:51.729
[S02] because they haven't learned how to negate the worst.

48
00:03:51.759 --> 00:03:55.030
[S02] So with that said, whether you've taken advantage of these or not,

49
00:03:55.060 --> 00:04:01.120
[S02] you have three superpowers at your disposal right now to take control of your future.

50
00:04:01.150 --> 00:04:03.310
[S02] And those are, one, learning.

51
00:04:03.340 --> 00:04:08.259
[S02] So the ability to adapt and figure out what actions you must take to get specific results.

52
00:04:08.289 --> 00:04:09.610
[S02] Two, persuasion.

53
00:04:09.639 --> 00:04:14.199
[S02] The ability to build trust and attract people to a mutually beneficial vision or narrative.

54
00:04:14.229 --> 00:04:18.100
[S02] We're talking about persuasion and mutual benefit, not manipulation.

55
00:04:18.129 --> 00:04:19.750
[S02] And three, execution.

56
00:04:19.779 --> 00:04:25.209
[S02] So the ability to turn ideas into reality through automation, creation, and delegation.

57
00:04:25.240 --> 00:04:29.860
[S02] So this creates a clear distinction between who will thrive and who won't.

58
00:04:29.889 --> 00:04:31.959
[S02] Doers versus directors.

59
00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:34.600
[S02] Employees versus entrepreneurs.

60
00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:37.060
[S02] Low agency versus high agency.

61
00:04:37.089 --> 00:04:41.050
[S02] Those who assign work rather than being assigned work.

62
00:04:41.079 --> 00:04:47.560
[S02] So you assigning the work either to yourself, self-direction, directing your own career,

63
00:04:47.589 --> 00:04:52.899
[S02] or to an AI prompt, or an automation tool, or something of that nature

64
00:04:52.930 --> 00:04:57.459
[S02] that allows you to do more and pursue your personal goals

65
00:04:57.490 --> 00:04:59.769
[S02] that bring more meaning and fulfillment to your life.

66
00:04:59.800 --> 00:05:01.329
[S02] So, free people.

67
00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:06.220
[S02] The definition of a free individual is those who pursue their interests and do many things throughout their lives.

68
00:05:06.250 --> 00:05:12.910
[S02] It's those that don't need permission to identify and solve a problem to create value in the world.

69
00:05:12.939 --> 00:05:17.079
[S02] And since new problems emerge as reality gets more complex

70
00:05:17.110 --> 00:05:23.259
[S02] and we're entering this period with AI and technology that is making reality very complex,

71
00:05:23.290 --> 00:05:25.629
[S02] there are more problems to solve than ever.

72
00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:29.199
[S02] There is more opportunities than ever for that reason alone.

73
00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:32.319
[S02] If you don't understand that problems are infinite,

74
00:05:32.350 --> 00:05:37.209
[S02] I would recommend you take a year and read "The Beginning of Infinity" by David Deutsch

75
00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:39.519
[S02] and truly study it and understand it.

76
00:05:39.550 --> 00:05:44.560
[S02] If you want kind of a summary of it, I talk about it in my free book, "Purpose and Profit."

77
00:05:44.589 --> 00:05:48.040
[S02] You can buy the paperback on Amazon, but on my Substack, it's free.

78
00:05:48.069 --> 00:05:55.420
[S02] So if problems are infinite, then solutions, or value, or wealth creation is also infinite.

79
00:05:55.449 --> 00:06:02.649
[S02] That means money is also infinite, but money is not limited to the definition of a US dollar, right?

80
00:06:02.680 --> 00:06:04.660
[S02] It's changing right now with Bitcoin technology.

81
00:06:04.689 --> 00:06:07.509
[S02] The reality is, in the past, it was seashells, right?

82
00:06:07.540 --> 00:06:09.100
[S02] You trade seashells, that was your money.

83
00:06:09.129 --> 00:06:11.259
[S02] In the future, we don't know what it's gonna be.

84
00:06:11.290 --> 00:06:16.180
[S02] It's gonna take the shape of culture and society, but it's still infinite

85
00:06:16.209 --> 00:06:20.350
[S02] because it represents value exchange, and that's innate to human nature.

86
00:06:20.379 --> 00:06:21.819
[S02] So we got that out of the way.

87
00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:23.829
[S02] You need to become a director.

88
00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:25.569
[S02] You need to become an orchestrator.

89
00:06:25.600 --> 00:06:27.220
[S02] You need to become high agency.

90
00:06:27.250 --> 00:06:32.139
[S02] You need to identify, solve problems, learn, build, all of the things that you would do

91
00:06:32.170 --> 00:06:38.860
[S02] if you were pursuing your own goals and didn't conform to the life that you were set on

92
00:06:38.889 --> 00:06:40.839
[S02] as a child by your parents and teachers.

93
00:06:40.870 --> 00:06:44.680
[S02] And the thing about that is that whether AI was coming into play or not,

94
00:06:44.709 --> 00:06:47.110
[S02] that's the best way to live.

95
00:06:47.129 --> 00:06:49.120
[S02] That's how you live as a free individual.

96
00:06:49.149 --> 00:06:55.089
[S02] So I don't understand why you're complaining if AI is coming in and kind of forcing people to do this

97
00:06:55.120 --> 00:06:59.470
[S02] if they want to thrive in the future, because that's how you thrive in the future either way.

98
00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:03.160
[S02] It doesn't frickin' matter if AI is the catalyst to that.

99
00:07:03.189 --> 00:07:05.740
[S02] AI is obviously the catalyst to that.

100
00:07:05.769 --> 00:07:11.069
[S02] "Oh, but Dan, AI just generates slop and it's gonna turn the internet into this wasteland, right?

101
00:07:11.100 --> 00:07:13.060
[S02] The dead internet is accelerating."

102
00:07:13.089 --> 00:07:15.069
[S02] And I've created an entire video on that.

103
00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:16.660
[S02] I don't think that's the case at all.

104
00:07:16.689 --> 00:07:22.750
[S02] The internet's been dead for a while now, AI or not, and there's still very much a living internet.

105
00:07:22.779 --> 00:07:24.639
[S02] So how do we get on that side?

106
00:07:24.670 --> 00:07:27.160
[S02] How do we get on the side of the living internet?

107
00:07:27.189 --> 00:07:30.279
[S02] We need to understand that taste is the new intelligence.

108
00:07:30.310 --> 00:07:35.439
[S02] Because there's a huge anti-AI crowd, and personally, I think they're gonna lose.

109
00:07:35.470 --> 00:07:37.120
[S02] I think that is the losing side.

110
00:07:37.149 --> 00:07:43.660
[S02] I do not think that you should be, like, you should identify as anti-AI.

111
00:07:43.689 --> 00:07:48.129
[S02] So as we continue moving toward the future and as technology continues to develop,

112
00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:54.879
[S02] there will be an uproar of emotional people who are identified and attached with the tools of the past.

113
00:07:54.910 --> 00:08:00.970
[S02] And these people will attempt to discredit those who start to integrate AI in their work,

114
00:08:01.000 --> 00:08:08.350
[S02] especially creative work, because people have a lot of pent-up emotions around what creativity is, what art is.

115
00:08:08.379 --> 00:08:14.350
[S02] And I think that is blocking their ability to see how AI actually plays a role.

116
00:08:14.379 --> 00:08:19.959
[S02] Because most people think that AI generations are just random, that you're just like, "Oh, I'm telling..."

117
00:08:19.990 --> 00:08:25.910
[S03] This AI to create a post and then I'm gonna post it and I'm gonna try to play that off as art.

118
00:08:25.930 --> 00:08:30.370
[S03] You're only seeing the surface. You genuinely do not understand it.

119
00:08:30.430 --> 00:08:34.649
[S03] And that's exactly why these people are so highly emotional, because it's just a principle, right?

120
00:08:34.669 --> 00:08:36.610
[S03] People hate what they don't understand.

121
00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:41.909
[S03] So my advice here is to ignore these people and do not become one of these people.

122
00:08:41.940 --> 00:08:45.600
[S03] Because maintaining a pessimistic mindset in general is bad.

123
00:08:45.629 --> 00:08:49.929
[S03] But over the coming years, it just creates this destructive confirmation bias.

124
00:08:49.950 --> 00:08:54.129
[S03] The people who are upset at me at this point in the video, which are gonna be quite a few people,

125
00:08:54.149 --> 00:08:56.830
[S03] are just reacting instantly to what I said.

126
00:08:56.850 --> 00:09:03.169
[S03] If they actually had a critique, if they actually had something reasonable to say, they wouldn't be so emotional.

127
00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:08.289
[S03] They would be able to respond with calmness and we would be able to have a good discussion about it.

128
00:09:08.309 --> 00:09:12.230
[S03] And we'd probably come to a solution where both of us are happy with it.

129
00:09:12.259 --> 00:09:14.309
[S03] But that's not how YouTube comments go.

130
00:09:14.399 --> 00:09:16.429
[S03] In general, you become what you focus on.

131
00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:21.830
[S03] And if you're trying to stop an avalanche from the bottom of a mountain, you're going to get crushed.

132
00:09:21.860 --> 00:09:28.289
[S03] What these people don't understand is that how we create is evolving, just like it has before.

133
00:09:28.320 --> 00:09:31.070
[S03] And these highly emotional people are nothing new.

134
00:09:31.100 --> 00:09:33.610
[S03] It happens during every technological shift.

135
00:09:33.639 --> 00:09:35.929
[S03] Think of photography or video.

136
00:09:36.070 --> 00:09:40.289
[S03] These are extremely recent developments on the timeline of humanity.

137
00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:46.470
[S03] I don't think you would argue that many people consider computer-generated graphics, CGI,

138
00:09:46.500 --> 00:09:51.169
[S03] or photo editing like Photoshop as an art form in one way or another.

139
00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:54.029
[S03] These things weren't able to be created in the past.

140
00:09:54.059 --> 00:10:02.629
[S03] You couldn't generate a hyper-realistic scene with computer-generated graphics in just a few clicks in the past.

141
00:10:02.659 --> 00:10:04.090
[S03] It wasn't even possible.

142
00:10:04.120 --> 00:10:10.269
[S03] And yes, it's difficult to create those things because you have to acquire the skill to do so.

143
00:10:10.299 --> 00:10:15.409
[S03] But the fact remains that it's a lot easier to do it than it was with the previous software.

144
00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:18.769
[S03] And it just keeps getting easier and easier and easier to do it.

145
00:10:18.799 --> 00:10:23.710
[S03] So just because a few clicks on your screen are being replaced with natural language,

146
00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:27.289
[S03] doesn't mean it's any easier to create something we see as art,

147
00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:33.529
[S03] especially when what we see as art changes as culture does and culture is shifting rapidly right now.

148
00:10:33.559 --> 00:10:38.370
[S03] We don't consider people artists for what specific tool they use.

149
00:10:38.399 --> 00:10:42.210
[S03] We consider people artists for why and how they use the tool.

150
00:10:42.309 --> 00:10:47.330
[S03] We grade them on the final product, the story, the emotion, the vision, the soul,

151
00:10:47.360 --> 00:10:50.210
[S03] not the hours of labor that went into creating it.

152
00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:54.669
[S03] You can spend ten years or ten seconds writing a book and call yourself an author,

153
00:10:54.700 --> 00:10:58.649
[S03] but that doesn't mean the book is good and especially doesn't mean that the book is art.

154
00:10:58.679 --> 00:11:02.250
[S03] So this is what the anti-AI crowd gets horribly wrong.

155
00:11:02.279 --> 00:11:06.230
[S03] They lump everyone using AI into the same category.

156
00:11:06.259 --> 00:11:08.169
[S03] If you use AI, they cut you off.

157
00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:13.570
[S03] There's actually people that do this, like imagine that, oh, my friend in high school uses ChatGPT.

158
00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:16.370
[S03] He's no longer my friend. Are you, are you serious?

159
00:11:16.399 --> 00:11:20.809
[S03] You've allowed a little chat box to control your mind that much and you are,

160
00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:26.289
[S03] you are angry at the person who you think is becoming dumber by using ChatGPT,

161
00:11:26.320 --> 00:11:29.809
[S03] but you just allowed it to do the same thing to your mind

162
00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:33.610
[S03] because you boxed yourself into this little ideology that AI is bad.

163
00:11:33.639 --> 00:11:35.610
[S03] That's how you become dumb.

164
00:11:35.639 --> 00:11:40.090
[S03] That's how you limit your ability to think and see nuance.

165
00:11:40.120 --> 00:11:44.730
[S03] So these anti-AI people just see AI as a chat box.

166
00:11:44.759 --> 00:11:47.929
[S03] They don't see any of the other implications of it.

167
00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:51.169
[S03] They just think it's a chat box where you write, "Write this newsletter for me,"

168
00:11:51.200 --> 00:11:54.070
[S03] or "Create this post for me," or "Create this image for me."

169
00:11:54.100 --> 00:11:57.850
[S03] And while some of the outputs of those may look visually appealing,

170
00:11:57.879 --> 00:12:00.929
[S03] that doesn't mean they're art and it doesn't mean they're useful

171
00:12:00.960 --> 00:12:03.210
[S03] and it doesn't mean they're gonna get traction on the internet.

172
00:12:03.240 --> 00:12:05.769
[S03] And if people flood the internet with these things,

173
00:12:06.039 --> 00:12:08.529
[S03] you have to understand what market sophistication is.

174
00:12:08.559 --> 00:12:10.529
[S03] Eventually, those things get boring.

175
00:12:10.559 --> 00:12:16.490
[S03] Eventually, they don't work and the pendulum swings to the other side and creators are biased once again.

176
00:12:16.519 --> 00:12:20.490
[S03] You're stuck with this doomsday mindset when that never happens.

177
00:12:20.519 --> 00:12:25.129
[S03] We don't get to a doomsday with all this stuff and AI isn't gonna change that either.

178
00:12:25.159 --> 00:12:30.850
[S03] If we think about it, this exposes something important in the anti-AI crowd.

179
00:12:30.879 --> 00:12:35.169
[S03] It exposes that most people have put too much emphasis on the labor

180
00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:40.009
[S03] that goes into creating something visually appealing so that it has become their identity

181
00:12:40.029 --> 00:12:44.610
[S03] and people will fight tooth and nail to avoid limiting themselves less.

182
00:12:44.629 --> 00:12:51.009
[S03] I personally do not believe that art should be tied to a series of buttons that people press,

183
00:12:51.029 --> 00:12:55.879
[S03] but instead, why they press that series of buttons, that's what art is.

184
00:12:55.909 --> 00:12:58.129
[S03] Now with all of this, I'm defending AI,

185
00:12:58.159 --> 00:13:03.529
[S03] but I think we can all agree that it is not smart to outsource all of your control

186
00:13:03.559 --> 00:13:06.250
[S03] and decision-making and work to AI.

187
00:13:06.279 --> 00:13:11.759
[S03] Yes, that's obviously a surefire way to become stupid, but that's not what everyone is doing.

188
00:13:11.789 --> 00:13:13.169
[S03] That's what stupid people are doing.

189
00:13:13.190 --> 00:13:17.649
[S03] That's both what stupid people are doing and what stupid people are focusing on.

190
00:13:17.679 --> 00:13:24.169
[S03] Neither of those parties see that AI has become a programming language for language itself.

191
00:13:24.190 --> 00:13:28.090
[S03] And when we're talking about AI here, we're talking about LLMs, right?

192
00:13:28.120 --> 00:13:32.649
[S03] There's much more than just LLMs, but for the sake of this, we're talking about those.

193
00:13:32.679 --> 00:13:37.169
[S03] Most people don't understand that prompt engineering is an art,

194
00:13:37.190 --> 00:13:41.610
[S03] that you can give hyper-specific instructions to an AI

195
00:13:41.629 --> 00:13:46.850
[S03] and it will conform to those instructions so that it's very close

196
00:13:46.879 --> 00:13:50.429
[S03] to being exactly what you would have done in the first place

197
00:13:50.460 --> 00:13:55.929
[S03] because that's what the Photoshop tools, the AI, the AE, After Effects, Premiere,

198
00:13:55.960 --> 00:13:57.210
[S03] that's what those allow you to do.

199
00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:03.529
[S03] All of the little buttons and nodules are just a way to make it more specific to the image that you're trying to create.

200
00:14:03.559 --> 00:14:06.450
[S03] So if you can't do that with natural language,

201
00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:11.669
[S03] one, you may have to go with a different medium, but you can do a lot with natural language.

202
00:14:11.700 --> 00:14:15.809
[S03] You can create what you wanna create, you just don't understand prompt engineering.

203
00:14:15.840 --> 00:14:19.870
[S03] And the thing here is that that's what most employed creative work is.

204
00:14:19.990 --> 00:14:26.440
[S04] Anyway, right? People are like, oh, I'm a photographer, I'm a video editor, I'm whatever it may be.

205
00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:30.509
[S04] But you're just executing project constructions that you didn't set, right?

206
00:14:30.539 --> 00:14:33.980
[S04] You are the doer, not the orchestrator, not the director.

207
00:14:34.009 --> 00:14:35.629
