00:21:00.250 --> 00:21:03.400
[S05] They're pairing with programmers, creators, coders.

305
00:21:03.430 --> 00:21:09.369
[S05] Now, of course, AI isn't directly responsible for this, but the general technological shift is.

306
00:21:09.400 --> 00:21:14.200
[S05] Another thing here is that even coders alone are understanding the need for distribution.

307
00:21:14.220 --> 00:21:17.799
[S05] And they're generating millions of views on social media by themselves.

308
00:21:17.849 --> 00:21:22.609
[S05] And by doing that, by stacking that skill, they just drastically increase their earning potential.

309
00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:26.500
[S05] They can both work for themselves on the projects that they want to work on.

310
00:21:26.740 --> 00:21:34.599
[S05] And maybe not immediately, but they can out earn, substantially out earn, any job they would have gotten.

311
00:21:34.690 --> 00:21:40.819
[S05] And I know there's going to be people where it's like, oh, well, Mark Zuckerberg paid a hundred million dollars to get the top AI researcher.

312
00:21:40.839 --> 00:21:42.500
[S05] You're not a top AI researcher.

313
00:21:42.740 --> 00:21:46.970
[S05] The peak of human ability lies in being a specialized generalist.

314
00:21:47.049 --> 00:21:52.670
[S05] Not so specialized that you get replaced in an instant or taken advantage of because you are identified with a credential.

315
00:21:52.750 --> 00:21:56.099
[S05] But not so generalized that you are effective at effectively nothing.

316
00:21:56.240 --> 00:21:59.599
[S05] Now, step two is to become a filter for ideas.

317
00:21:59.630 --> 00:22:03.200
[S05] Because ideas now matter more than execution.

318
00:22:03.299 --> 00:22:06.670
[S05] They they're not the only thing that matters, obviously, but they matter more.

319
00:22:06.750 --> 00:22:07.140
[S05] Why?

320
00:22:07.230 --> 00:22:11.299
[S05] Because if taste is the new intelligence and curation is the new IQ test.

321
00:22:11.329 --> 00:22:15.170
[S05] In a world where information is overflowing and anyone can create anything.

322
00:22:15.269 --> 00:22:20.269
[S05] Choosing what ideas deserve to be executed deserves the first seat at the table.

323
00:22:20.349 --> 00:22:21.299
[S05] And that's the thing.

324
00:22:21.329 --> 00:22:24.930
[S05] It's never been easier to create something mediocre.

325
00:22:25.130 --> 00:22:29.299
[S05] So people that think, oh, I should just pump out as much content as possible.

326
00:22:29.319 --> 00:22:32.000
[S05] And I can have chat GPT and automations do all these things.

327
00:22:32.430 --> 00:22:36.500
[S05] You're gonna lose or you're not gonna have any longevity in the space.

328
00:22:36.519 --> 00:22:42.170
[S05] Because those things change and you're not gonna understand the principles that allow you to continue to get results.

329
00:22:42.230 --> 00:22:45.230
[S05] Another thing is that UBI or universal basic income.

330
00:22:45.250 --> 00:22:50.500
[S05] You know, everybody getting a certain amount of money deposited into their bank account each month.

331
00:22:50.569 --> 00:22:57.400
[S05] Some people argue that everyone will have more time so that they can self actualize and become spiritual and transcend and all of this other stuff.

332
00:22:57.569 --> 00:23:01.799
[S05] While other people argue that everyone will just become mediocre and lazy.

333
00:23:01.829 --> 00:23:07.670
[S05] And the truth here is that it's just a deliberate personal choice, which one you become.

334
00:23:07.700 --> 00:23:13.099
[S05] Ninety nine percent of people, as always, have just proven that they will become lazy and mediocre.

335
00:23:13.230 --> 00:23:18.930
[S05] That's the bottom of the barrel, whether it be because of UBI and what they do with their life.

336
00:23:19.000 --> 00:23:23.970
[S05] Or those that choose to create mediocre content because it's so easy to do so.

337
00:23:24.000 --> 00:23:27.500
[S05] If there's no friction there, it's going to get old quick.

338
00:23:27.529 --> 00:23:34.000
[S05] And the people who are actually deliberate about being good and pursuing excellence are going to rise to the top.

339
00:23:34.029 --> 00:23:37.970
[S05] So your mind must become a finely tuned signal to noise filter.

340
00:23:38.000 --> 00:23:43.599
[S05] And for that to happen, you must understand that your mind interprets reality based on your identity and worldview.

341
00:23:43.630 --> 00:23:50.000
[S05] Your values and beliefs, the code you live by, your level of development, the vision you are attempting to actualize.

342
00:23:50.170 --> 00:23:54.930
[S05] Your brain releases dopamine when information is useful toward your main and overarching goals.

343
00:23:54.950 --> 00:23:56.769
[S05] We've talked about this so many different times.

344
00:23:56.799 --> 00:24:04.130
[S05] Go look at my top videos or any of the videos where I talk about reinventing yourself or taking back control of your life.

345
00:24:04.150 --> 00:24:05.599
[S05] We talk about all of that.

346
00:24:05.670 --> 00:24:10.230
[S05] So in order to develop taste or become a filter for signal and noise.

347
00:24:10.250 --> 00:24:13.569
[S05] You have to have your own point of view that you're cultivating.

348
00:24:13.599 --> 00:24:21.569
[S05] You have to have your own philosophy, your own vision, because that's what frames your mind to notice the unique ideas that are worth putting out into the world.

349
00:24:21.670 --> 00:24:25.170
[S05] Under your own philosophy, under your own brand.

350
00:24:25.269 --> 00:24:28.869
[S05] Now, step three is to become an AI orchestrator.

351
00:24:28.900 --> 00:24:35.329
[S05] Because the first and foundational decision to make when adopting AI lies in when to leverage it and when to do it by hand.

352
00:24:35.430 --> 00:24:38.730
